{
  "requirement": "The `{term}` role followed by a backtick-delimited full definition name MUST reference a named definition. In rendered Markdown, the reference MUST display the definition's phrase as an ordinary hyperlink to its anchored declaration, without a badge. The phrase MUST be escaped appropriately for rendering.\n\nMissing targets and targets of another kind MUST be reported. Escaped references and references in Markdown code examples MUST remain literal. A term reference MUST NOT satisfy a requirement citation or a Rust function's requirement-coverage obligation.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "## Judgment: `requirements.definitions.references` is satisfied\n\nThe implementation covers each obligation and the tests cover the main cases. The one weakness is that two helpers contribute to term rendering without an `{impl}` marker for this requirement, which I judged minor.\n\n### Implementation\n\n- **Recognizing `{term}` references.** `reference_roles` in `src/lib.rs` recognizes `{term}` followed by a backtick-delimited name.\n  - It accepts only valid full names.\n  - It skips backslash-escaped roles through `is_escaped`.\n  - It skips Markdown code examples (code spans, code blocks, raw HTML) through `protected_ranges` and `is_protected`.\n  - Those literals stay literal and are not counted as references.\n- **Rendering.** `replace_inline_references` (`src/lib.rs`) turns a `{term}` whose target is a term definition into `<a href=\"…#habeas-definition-<name>\">phrase</a>`.\n  - The anchor comes from `DefinitionKind::anchor`.\n  - The phrase comes from `terms::declaration` and is escaped by `terms::escaped_phrase`. That function turns Markdown-significant characters into numeric entities and HTML-escapes the rest.\n  - No badge is produced, and `reference_badge` explicitly returns `None` for term targets.\n  - `replace_definitions` renders the declaration as `<strong id=\"habeas-definition-…\">`, which is the anchored declaration the link targets.\n  - `preprocess_book` drives this over all chapters, including detail pages.\n- **Reporting bad targets.** `validate_references` in `src/evidence.rs` reports `term X has no matching named definition` when the target is missing or is a requirement or example (another kind).\n  - It also reports non-term roles that point at a term or at nothing.\n- **Terms never count as citations or coverage.** A term reference is never added to `by_definition`, so it cannot satisfy evidence coverage or any citation.\n  - `missing_function_references` (`src/rust_coverage.rs`) returns `false` for `ReferenceKind::Term`, and `DefinitionKind::Term` targets never count.\n  - `example_references_requirement` accepts only `Cite` and `Test`.\n  - The authoring inputs and `reference_selector` code ignore terms.\n  - `terms::referenced_terms` handles dependencies separately.\n\n### Tests\n\n- `phrases_are_plain_links_and_markdown_literals_are_preserved` checks the rendering case.\n  - It asserts the declaration anchor and that no `shields.io` badge appears.\n  - It asserts exactly one link, with the correct relative cross-chapter href from a nested chapter.\n  - It asserts the escaped phrase `&#42;alpha&#42; &amp; &lt;beta&gt;`.\n  - Together with the exactly-one-link count, this shows the code-span, fenced-code and backslash-escaped forms stay unlinked.\n- `invalid_declarations_and_reference_targets_are_reported` checks that a term pointing at a requirement (`rules.ready`) and a term pointing at a missing name are both reported.\n- `definition_schema_deletion_and_literal_references_are_checked` checks several cases.\n  - Deleting the glossary produces the missing-target error.\n  - Literal forms (code span, fence, escape) yield no validation errors and no inputs.\n- `outline_inputs_capture_terms_without_counting_them_as_citations` checks that a Rust comment holding only a `{term}` leaves the function reported as having \"no requirement or example citation\".\n- The `validate_references` test for unknown `{impl}` targets and the `{test}`-marked tests for `references.markdown_literals` add coverage of the other-role failure case and the literal rules.\n\n### Gaps and caveats\n\n- `chapter_href` and `replace_references` contribute to term-link generation and nested fenced bodies, but they carry `{impl}` markers only for book-badge and navigation requirements, not this one. They are shared helpers, and I judged this minor.\n- There is no explicit test for a same-chapter term link, a term reference inside a fenced-reference body, or a term pointing at an example. The code paths are the same, so I judged this acceptable.\n- There are no FIXMEs.",
  "references": "9dd0d49ec24fc8b569c1a8d77409ce793041e85bbbf5de66fb2ce6d244585def",
